{
  "title": "Six preselected historic mast-registration spot checks and one retired-source control",
  "coverage": "Not the geometry of 74 separately surveyed turbines. Two points each selected by fixed quarter points of the major geographic axis of the full positive-capacity federal roster, not chosen for a good return or rendered angle. Seventh point was excluded before looking at LiDAR.",
  "preregisterSHA256": "0b7fcdc99c49ca0c240892a5d9aaf9dd575055364b0e2ef338e647ef58f18891",
  "federalRosterSHA256": "be47c773727d66bba2c25ebe5325341d56c74b1c6a66d6a2163ce4a03f743c61",
  "sourceObjectProject": "NRCan CanElevation \u2013 Prince Edward Island 2020. Original federal point classification (mostly unclassified material high above grade, not a class-name assumption that returns are a turbine); not another survey or a current operational feed.",
  "method": "Ground: nonwithheld class 2 last returns between 10m and 22m of source, equal vote per 2.5m cell, 12-iteration robust affine fit, minimum 20 cells/3 quadrants and P90 <=0.70m. Deduplicate by flight line, scanner channel, time, return number across exact bounded source objects. Require >=2 original distinct nonnoise laser pulses within 3m of registry and >20m above grade. Near-hub band is +/-8m, diagnostic only. Full 23m circular national catalogue coverage is reported separately from affirmative direct structure proximity.",
  "interpretation": "2020 returns support a tall structure at all six sampled positive-capacity points, including many within +/-8m of registered nominal hub. Eastern Kings and Hermanville pass both full-disk geometry gates at both samples. West Cape has positive direct near-hub observations at both, but the catalogue union omits small outer portions (11.45 and 26.48m\u00b2); retain that qualification. A separately registered direct XY-only check corroborates both 3m cores; it does not make either whole 23m circle complete to 1m. The 2020 historical observation at later-retired HCS9 is not authority for showing a present turbine there.",
